> Here are excerpts of an article:
> ===
> Does global warming affect unemployment? International evidence
> Liu & Lin 2023
> 
> Abstract
> A global perspective is taken to explore the effect of global warming on the unemployment rate in 95 countries from 1992–2020. The robust results show that global warming raises the unemployment rate through the inflation rate, agricultural production and urbanization. The results also show that the unemployment rate increases with global warming in countries at latitudes between 20 and 40 degrees, while a negative effect exists at latitudes greater than 40 degrees. Additionally, global warming has a significant effect on male unemployment but not on female unemployment. Furthermore, global warming has a significant positive effect on unemployment in middle-income countries compared to its effect in low- and high-income countries. Our research has value as a reference for the economic impacts of global climate change.
> Introduction
> This paper is the first to discuss the effect of global warming on unemployment from a quantitative and international perspective. The causes of unemployment have long been a major concern of economic scholars (Kocaarslan et al., 2020, Azzollini, 2023, Bianchi et al., 2023), while there is a lack of quantitative research on the impact of global warming on labor employment. Unemployment is affected not only by the dynamic nature of the labor market, asymmetry, the current economic state, etc., but also by the impact of natural factors caused by global warming or extreme weather. With the intensification of human production activities and the advancement of industrialization processes, we cannot eliminate our dependence on traditional energy in the short term, despite the fact that the large amount of carbon emissions from traditional energy use strongly contribute to global warming. The gradual trend of global temperature rise has had a series of impacts on labor flow and employment, the natural environment, and social conditions, which in turn affect labor unemployment (Huang et al., 2020). On the other hand, unemployment, as a macroeconomic barometer and a key index of people’s livelihood, has long been an important macro variable of interest to the academic community and the government. Unemployment is related to people’s livelihood and well-being and the stability of the economy and politics (Azzollini, 2023). Therefore, the study of unemployment affected by global warming has great practical significance for understanding the changes in labor market supply and demand caused by climate change.
> Global warming will have an impact on the production process and final output of the economic system, not only on agriculture and the living environment but also on the industrialization process in the long run (Simeonova and Diaz-Bone, 2005). The rising temperature may increase agricultural production area and potentially increase labor employment for some countries, while it may cause adjustments in industrial structure and lead to unemployment for other countries (Zheng et al., 2023). Previous studies on the factors influencing unemployment have ignored the impact of natural factors, especially climate change (Arestis et al., 2023, Jung and Pyun, 2023). There is still a lack of sufficient quantitative evidence on the impact of global warming on unemployment rates and regional heterogeneity across countries, which reduces the efficiency of climate control policies. However, this paper focuses on the causal heterogeneity and intermediary mechanism of global warming on the unemployment rate; an understanding of these factors is conducive to strengthening the macrostructural adjustment of the supply–demand relationship for the labor market. It also helps to optimize and adjust the national economic structure and provide references for formulating more scientific and specific carbon reduction and energy consumption policies across countries in the world.
> In recent years, an increasing number of countries have suffered from global warming. At the 21st United Nations Climate Change Conference in December 2015, representatives of 195 countries and regional organizations worldwide adopted the Paris Agreement, aiming to control global warming to no more than 2 °C by the end of this century and confine the magnitude of the temperature increase to no more than 1.5 °C (United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Chang, 2015). According to the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA), July 3, 2023, was the hottest day recorded, with the global average temperature reaching 17.01 °C at 2 m above the Earth’s surface.2 High-temperature weather will not only increase the possibility of rainstorms, floods, forest fires and extreme weather but also change the structure of economic production activities, especially the labor market. The temperature rise may induce both frictional unemployment and structural unemployment. In the short term, some jobs may be affected by high-temperature weather, leading to factors such as employee turnover and seasonal labor shortages (Roson and Van der Mensbrugghe, 2012). People may also be affected by high-temperature weather, with an increase in search costs for jobs, which leads to an increase in the probability of unemployment (Kjellstrom et al., 2009). In the long run, global warming may lead to a decrease in labor productivity in some industries, such as agriculture and mining, as well as an increase in the cost of labor engaged in these professions, resulting in a long-term downturn in industry employment rates (Dun et al., 2023).
> According to the Sixth Assessment Report on Climate Change (AR6) released by the IPCC, the global surface temperature increased by 1.1 °C from 2011–2020 compared to the temperature from 1850–1900 (IPCC, 2021). Temperature rise often induces extreme weather and natural disasters and can have a serious impact on people’s production space, energy structure and macroeconomy (Donadelli et al., 2017). Labor employment is related to the economic and political stability and well-being of a country, and global warming affects unemployment and the labor market structure, mainly in the following three aspects. First, global warming alters the production and operation scale of the agricultural industry and the energy utilization structure of traditional energy-intensive industries. The agricultural cultivation area and output may decrease due to temperature changes, thereby affecting agricultural labor employment. Studies have shown that large-scale carbon dioxide emissions caused by human activities are among the important factors in global warming (James and Menzies, 2022). The oil and coal industries, which emit many greenhouse gases, are expected to shed jobs. The carbon reduction in the heavy chemical industry due to industrial and energy structure adjustments may cause short-term unemployment of the labor force (Xiao and Liu, 2023). Second, the increase in regional extreme weather and sea level rise caused by global warming may induce the spatial movement and redistribution of labor resources and lead to the structural imbalance of the regional labor force in a certain period, thus inducing structural unemployment (Black et al., 2011). Third, the emergence of global warming and extreme weather may have a long-term impact on worker health and thereby a long-term impact on the workforce, especially in occupations in areas with environmental exposure and high temperatures (Teasdale and Panegyres, 2023).
> In fact, global warming may have heterogeneous impacts on economic growth and further impacts on labor employment. Hsiang (2010) finds that the loss of nonagricultural production caused by rising temperatures greatly exceeds the loss of agriculture. Additionally, global warming has a greater impact on the economic development of poor countries, which may lead to a widening wealth gap between countries. Furthermore, under the trend of global warming, the economic impact caused by temperature increases is less than 10% for mid-latitude countries and other countries with temperate climates (Diffenbaugh and Burke, 2019), while sensitive areas, such as those at high latitudes, are significantly more affected than areas at low latitudes (Kingsolver et al., 2013).
> Regarding the factors influencing labor unemployment, previous studies consider the macro perspective. Unel (2020) concludes that deregulation of the banking sector reduced unemployment by increasing the average job search rate. Silveira and Lima (2021) argue that there is a significant positive relationship between pessimistic unemployment expectations and actual unemployment. Hashimoto et al. (2023) argue that there is a labor paradox in the labor market, whereby an increase in aggregate demand improves the unemployment rate, while an increase in labor productivity exacerbates underemployment but does not improve the unemployment rate. Hegelund and Taalbi (2023) argue that unemployment is negatively related to the long-run component of investment. Monacelli et al. (2023) find that borrowing in financial markets affects employment through the “debt bargaining channel” and that the debt negotiation channel accounts for 26% of unemployment fluctuations. The mood of workers in the labor market can also have an impact on the unemployment rate.
> Moreover, studies have demonstrated that human economic activities are among the main factors of global warming and have focused on the factors influencing carbon emissions or energy efficiency that lead to global warming (Nel and Cooper, 2009, Sufyanullah et al., 2022, Santos, 2022, Song et al., 2023, Wang and Lee, 2023). Santos (2022) considers the improvement of the carbon pricing ceiling to be an important measure for achieving carbon reduction and curbing the risk of global warming. Lee and Zhao (2023) find that population and technology significantly improve CO2 emissions. Song et al. (2023) suggest that carbon trading improves energy efficiency by adjusting the energy structure and promoting green technology innovation. However, few studies have examined the influence of global warming on unemployment (Desmet and Rossi-Hansberg, 2015, Fernando et al., 2021, Cevik and Jalles, 2023). Regarding the economic effect of climate change, more studies have addressed the agricultural industry. Mideksa (2010) concludes that climate change has reduced agricultural output and increased income inequality in Ethiopia, thus further reducing economic growth and increasing poverty. Ali et al. (2021) show that the impact of increased summer temperatures on net income ranged from US$29 to US$35 per hectare, and the impact of increased winter temperatures on net income ranged from US$14 to US$28 per hectare. Duan et al. (2022) argue that an increase of 1 °C in temperature will result in an output reduction of 0.78% in China. Abeysekara et al. (2023) find that global climate change will decrease most crop production in Sri Lanka, which will increase the consumer prices of these agricultural products and cause total household consumption to decline in the coming decades. Climate change will also have an impact on water resources, and Eamen et al. (2020) suggest that taking appropriate measures to address climate change-induced water scarcity can reduce economic losses by almost 50%.
> Regarding the labor market affected by climate change, Babiker and Eckaus (2007) argue that the effects of restricting GHG emissions on unemployment cannot be eliminated but that the negative effects of restricting carbon emissions can be ameliorated and possibly eliminated if offset policies, such as wage subsidies, are implemented. Matsumoto finds (2019) that climate change has particularly significant impacts on high-temperature regions, but positive economic effects are gained for some regions in the middle- and low-temperature zones due to comparative advantages resulting from interregional differences in labor productivity changes. Huang et al. (2020) show that the time spent by rural residents on agricultural production will decrease by 7.0% when the temperature rises by 1 °C, while the time spent on nonagricultural industries will increase by 7.8%, and leisure time will decrease by 0.8%. Mueller et al. (2020) find that climate anomalies, such as rising temperatures and precipitation, affect population migration. Liu et al. (2023b) argue that continuous improvements from using sustainable technologies in manufacturing and construction can contribute to decoupling CO2 emissions and stabilizing employment in society more than the transition from industry to the tertiary sector.
> The contributions of this article are as follows. First, this paper is the first in which discuss the effects of global warming on unemployment are discussed from a quantitative perspective. In most studies, only the influence of global warming on economic output, labor flow or product trade from a single country perspective is addressed; only in a few studies are the extent and direction of the impact of global warming on unemployment given attention from a global perspective (Graff Zivin and Neidell, 2014, Fankhauser and Tol, 2005, Burke et al., 2018). Nearly 30 years of historical data from 95 countries worldwide are used to assess the impact of temperature change on the national unemployment rate, which fills the gap in the study of the labor employment effect of climate change.
> Second, the two-stage least squares method based on instrumental variables are adopted to analyze the impact of global temperature change on unemployment and its impact mechanism. Endogenous econometric analysis is rarely considered in previous studies on the economic impact of global warming, and the traditional OLS estimation method does not account for the bidirectional causal effect of labor unemployment and temperature rise and other endogeneity problems, such as possible missing variables, which distort the results (Theil, 1953, Basmann, 1957). In this paper, the product of the share of clean energy use and the country latitude value is introduced as an instrumental variable to solve the endogeneity problem and obtains robust results indicating that global temperature change has a significant positive impact on the unemployment rate, with the overall unemployment rate rising by 3.4% for every 1°C increase in global temperature change above the baseline. Additionally, we find that rising temperatures have an impact on unemployment by reducing inflation, urbanization and agricultural production space as well as by increasing economic growth.
> Third, previous studies have not addressed the heterogeneous characteristics of the influence of global warming on unemployment. In our study, we consider heterogeneity from the perspectives of geographical location, gender structure and economic development degree. The results show that the temperature change has a significant effect on male unemployment but not on female unemployment. Additionally, global warming causes the unemployment rate to decrease if the latitude quantile is greater than 40 degrees, while it has a positive effect when the latitude quantile is between 20 and 40 degrees. Moreover, temperature change has a significant positive effect on unemployment in middle-income countries compared to countries with lower and higher levels of income.
> The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section 2 presents the empirical background on global warming and unemployment. Section 3 proposes the hypotheses. Section 4 shows the IV-2SLS method. Section 5 presents the empirical results. Section 6 presents the conclusions and suggestions.
