> Global warming significantly impacts unemployment rates through various mechanisms, including inflation and disruptions in economic activity, agricultural output, and urbanization. As temperatures rise, economic disturbances may lead to declining asset values and business activities, which can increase unemployment due to deflationary pressures. The study by Liu & Lin (2023) highlights that countries located between 20 and 40 degrees latitude experience higher unemployment linked to global warming, while regions above 40 degrees may see a reduction in unemployment due to potential agricultural expansion. Furthermore, the effects of climate change vary across economic contexts, with middle-income countries bearing a greater burden and male unemployment being more affected than female unemployment. Long-term implications such as decreased labor productivity and increased labor costs in certain sectors further complicate this dynamic. Consequently, it is essential for policymakers to develop adaptive economic policies that address these climate-induced changes in unemployment dynamics to ensure resilient financial and labor systems.
